// @check
// @error-at 30:32 argument 1: expected string, got i32
// @error-at 34:30 argument 1: expected i32, got string
// @error-at 47:34 argument 1: expected string, got _[]
// SOUNDNESS. A function whose returns JOIN across an `is` guard over its own
// un-annotated param mixes a CONCRETE branch type with a hole-DERIVED one:
//
//     function foobar(v) {
//       if v is {foo: string} then return v.foo   // `string` (narrowed binding)
//       return v.bar                              // `?fld.bar.?foobar.0`
//     }
//
// A hole is permissively assignable both ways, so the join's mutual-assignability
// collapse silently DROPPED the hole and pinned the whole return to `string`. The
// call site then had nothing left to resolve — `substHoleByName` never saw the
// field hole — and an i32 return flowed into a `string` parameter with NO
// diagnostic at all, emitting invalid wasm ("expected (ref $type), found i32").
// The no-guard spelling (`function getbar(v) { return v.bar }`) always rejected
// this; the guard was the only way past it.
//
// The join now KEEPS both sides and the call site re-folds them under its pinned
// argument, so each spelling below reports the field's real type at the argument
// position. `inference/hole-is-guard-return-join.vl` is the must-not-error twin.
function printString(thing: string) { print(thing) }
function printI32(thing: i32) { print(thing) }

function foobar(v) {
  if v is { foo: string } then return v.foo
  return v.bar
}
printString(foobar({ bar: 42 })) // the complement path returns i32

// The mirror: the GUARD branch's type is the one that cannot flow. `{foo: string}`
// never reaches `v.bar`, so this call returns `string`.
printI32(foobar({ foo: "s" }))

// `else`-arm spelling of the same guard, and an argument whose field type the
// emitter previously could not even monomorphize ("unsupported argument type for
// `v`") — a low-quality emit failure for what is a type error at the argument.
// The `got` half is pinned WHOLE (`_[]`) rather than cut before the type: the
// argument is an empty `[]`, so its element is a type nothing constrained, and the
// renderer's blank is what names it. A pin that stopped at `got` could not see which
// token stood there, which is how the internal `<none>` spelling survived here.
function either(v) {
  if v is { left: i32 } then return v.left
  else return v.right
}
printString(either({ right: [] }))
